    hints: ['Visit each value and check it against both bounds.', 'Use value >= lower and value <= upper, or the equivalent chained comparison.', 'Append only when both checks pass. Move the position on every turn.'],
    explanation: 'A filter decides whether to append each original value. It neither sorts nor removes duplicates. and expresses that both bounds must hold. The output can be shorter than the input, so a separate output-position counter is unnecessary: append handles the next slot.',
    solution: 'result = []\nposition = 0\nwhile position < len(values):\n    value = values[position]\n    if value >= lower and value <= upper:\n        result.append(value)\n    position = position + 1\n',
